e fit calculation already reports them.\n\n## Waiting on the API\nThe `next` branch shows the modifier lines that stack on one stat, using `parts`, which the live API already sends. It also uses two new help-text fields (`statLabel`/`statValue`) that the live API doesn't send yet. Holding `next` until the API ships them is what your workflow says to do, so that's correct.\n\n## Game rules hardcoded because the API doesn't provide them\n- **Grid rotation and port rules** ([geometry.ts](src/components/grid/geometry.ts)) are copied from the game's C# code, and only tests compare them against tables copied from Unity. If the game changes rotations, the builder's drag preview will disagree with the verdict until someone re-syncs. The API still re-checks every fit, so the damage stays small.\n- **Which row heads each Hull Specs section** (`HEADLINES` in [hull-specs.tsx](src/components/fit/hull-specs.tsx)) is a fixed map from section to row id. If the API renames a row, that header quietly goes blank.\n- **Ship class and role lists** are built from the hulls. The API's `/v1/ship-classes` and `/v1/ship-roles` give the proper order, and the classes route includes class icons.\n\nThe first two would be better coming from the API, for example through `/v1/ui/fitting-grid` and a flag on each spec row. Both you and the API live in your monorepo, so that's a small change on its side.\n\n## Fine to ignore\n- **Roll math fields** (`pointRating`, `tierShares` and similar), because `/v1/roll` does that work on the server.\n- **NPCs, death causes, kills, battles and leaderboards**, which are killboard data rather than theorycrafting.\n\nComparing fields covered every catalog route against live responses; for the POST endpoints I only checked the API's published schemas. I'd fix regalia set bonuses first, then battery abilities, then scope. Want me to start on those?",
